What si-rpg-engine changed, 2026-09-26:

- Rapier's own fix, pull request https://github.com/dimforge/rapier/pull/1004
  (commit bd7a2f2e, released in rapier 0.36.0). Each dynamic collider's
  contact manifolds are computed into a vec of their own, then moved onto the
  shared list with that collider's body, normal, and pose. At 0.35.3 every
  collider in range shares one vec and the routine assumes parry appends to
  it; for a convex pair parry writes into the vec's first manifold instead,
  so with two dynamic bodies near the character the second body's manifold
  replaces the first's, and the first body is pushed at the second's points.
- The push's mass, which Rapier has not fixed. The report upstream is
  https://github.com/dimforge/rapier/issues/1020, open at rapier 0.36.0.
  For each contact point Rapier's routine sizes the impulse with the two
  bodies' linear masses only, m*M/(m+M), and leaves out the pushed body's
  rotation. A push at a point off the body's centre of mass turns it as well
  as moving it, so the impulse overshoots the velocity it aims for, and for a
  light body pushed far from its centre it launches the body. The copy sizes
  the impulse with the effective mass at the point, 1/(1/m + 1/M + k), where
  k = (r x n) . I^-1 (r x n) for the point's offset r from the body's world
  centre of mass, the contact normal n, and the body's world inverse inertia
  I^-1, all read from the body's public mass properties. With k exactly 0 the
  ratio is Rapier's bit for bit.
- The routine is adapted to Rapier's public API: its methods live on a
  wrapper of KinematicCharacterController, the bodies of a private method and
  a crate-private function are local functions, and a crate-private field is
  read through its public accessor. The profiling attributes are removed.
  Nothing else it computes is changed.
